; A TYPENAME as the range START bound (`uint..10`): both bounds are read as literal VALUES, before
; the head name is resolved at all, so a named bound has no value to lower onto the backing
; primitive. Refused gracefully, naming the bound. Name-INDEPENDENT — the same refusal fires for
; every head, supported names included — so it is a SHAPE-axis row, which is why the refused-name
; closure sweep (`refused_name_closure_tests`) keeps this column `ContextOwned` with a `Refuses`
; verdict rather than sweeping it. The END bound is its sibling row
; (`tests/robustness/rangeop_typename_end.cddl`), so the class and not the instance is pinned.
x = uint..10
